 colorful and bold so I just  took the plunge about 13 years ago and  changed my entire direction even before  that Matthew you were you were actually  working at Christie's and studying law  and so what what turned you to food then  well I moved to New York planning to go  to law school after college worked at  Christie's and I would go on my lunch  breaks everybody else would go to a cafe  and have lunch I would walk around  looking at restaurants reading menus  that was just addicted to  yeah something about restaurants was  calling me and I just loved the idea a  platform where you can be so creative on  creating design the atmosphere music  food and it just was a way to be social  and a little bit of an introvert it was  a way to be very social without being  fully exposed so I I just loved the idea  of it  and when you did get into the restaurant  world you trained in some very  prestigious institutions you yourself  have opened up a raw food Culinary  Institute tell us about how that came  about we opened the first state  accredited culinary school in 2009 and  now we have schools permanent or  temporary in New York LA Miami  Sydney's Paris Stockholm London Wow  Berlin coming Tokyo so we're doing  schools all over the world and online  we've had several thousand students  graduate we're trying to teach them how  to to do exactly what we do which is  create healthy food that actually tastes  good and will help people eat better  more and more chefs are embracing  vegetarian menus do you think dining  attitudes have changed dramatically I  mean if you look in even in New York for  example which may be a little behind LA  in terms of people embracing plant-based  jean-georges Vongerichten just too open  I know he's been on show he just opened  ABC V which is a vegetarian is no stop  by there after this and and you know  it's it's packed and all the new healthy  places are packed and I think it's  changing very quickly I think it's a  major tipping point happening where  plant centric will be the norm not you  know something that's just once once in  a while what about the business of it in  terms of sourcing the the vegetables and  the fruits and the necessary ingredients  is that landscape changed it has there  are products on the market now that  weren't even on the market five or six  years ago and I started Planet base 12  or 13 years ago you couldn't even find  rock cow beans and you know now they're  everywhere even probably Publix in Miami  so products are there farmers markets  are proliferating everywhere people are  more aware of organic and local and  sustainable it all starts with the  product that's the most important you  have seven restaurants now you're  opening to more we're opening in London  and we're opening another one in  California at four in California la New  York how's it consumer different in  London New York or  California uh you know California so  health friendly but New York double zero  we went with pizza instead of you know  more raw high-end food because we  thought you know New York it needs an  all year-round place it's worked really  well double zero has been pulled since  we open  Miami's been an incredible market for us  people are really embracing it we didn't  expect that because it was so such a  culture of enjoying meat and Anisa yeah  but it's really been like our one of our  best markets those beach bods vegan food  is good for that right um  as I asked you to sign the dish as we  customarily do on the show I will ask  you to answer the question we always  pose which is if you could share this  meal with anyone past or present who  would you share it with chef it would be  my parents I want to always give them as  much healthy food as possible so that's  a very sweet thing to do as a son as a  delicious thing to do Chef Matthew Kenny  thank you and for more on Matthew Kenny  and the dish head to our website at CBS  this morning dot-com
 

